                <h2>Don't Rush to Appeal - First Understand Why</h2>
<p>Before preparing appeal materials, <strong>identify the root cause of suspension</strong>. Google Ads suspensions typically fall into three categories: policy violations, payment issues, and suspected fraud. Different causes require different appeal strategies.</p>
<h3>✅ Step 1: Check Your Account for Suspension Details</h3>
<p>Log into Google Ads and locate the "Account Status" section. Google usually provides violation descriptions. If notification emails are missing, check:<br />
- Warning icons (yellow/red) in the upper right corner<br />
- Notifications under the "Issues" menu<br />
- Your registered email inbox</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Checklist</strong>: Have you read the full suspension notification? Have you taken screenshots? (To avoid losing the original notification during the appeal process)</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Appeal Materials Checklist</h2>
<h3>📋 Essential Documents (Required)</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Document</th>
<th>Purpose</th>
<th>Key Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Business license/Registration</td>
<td>Prove legal entity</td>
<td>Must be current; translate to English</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Website URL &amp; hosting info</td>
<td>Verify landing page compliance</td>
<td>Ensure all pages are accessible, no 404 errors</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Domain registration</td>
<td>Confirm website ownership</td>
<td>WHOIS info must match account details</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Advertiser ID</td>
<td>For personal accounts</td>
<td>Passport or national ID scan</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>📋 Documents for Policy Violations</h3>
<p>If suspended for ad policy violations, additionally prepare:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Complete landing page screenshots</strong>: Including full purchase flow, return policy, contact information</li>
<li><strong>Product/Service compliance statement</strong>: Confirm compliance with local laws (e.g., GDPR, local advertising regulations)</li>
<li><strong>Third-party certifications</strong>: Industry qualifications, product quality certifications, safety test reports</li>
<li><strong>Revised ad copy</strong>: Showing deleted violating content</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Checklist</strong>: Are all landing pages accessible? Do product descriptions match ad promises?</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>📋 Documents for Payment Issues</h3>
<ul>
<li>Front and back of credit/debit card (middle numbers can be masked)</li>
<li>Bank statement (showing account holder name)</li>
<li>Payment account screenshot (showing normal account status)</li>
<li>Authorization letter (if a third party pays on your behalf)</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step-by-Step Appeal Submission</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Fix Issues Before Appealing</h3>
<p><strong>Resolve obvious violations before submitting appeal</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>Delete or modify violating ads</li>
<li>Pause problematic landing pages</li>
<li>Update non-compliant website content</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 2: Access the Appeal Form</h3>
<pre><code>Google Ads Help Center → &quot;Contact us&quot; → Select account → Appeal option
</code></pre>
<p>Or directly visit: <code>support.google.com/google-ads/contact/apl_appeals</code></p>
<h3>Step 3: Write Your Appeal Statement</h3>
<p>The appeal statement is crucial. Structure it as follows:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Acknowledge the issue</strong>: State which policy was violated</li>
<li><strong>Explain the context</strong>: Briefly describe the background (avoid making excuses)</li>
<li><strong>List corrective actions</strong>: What you've already fixed</li>
<li><strong>Prevention plan</strong>: How you'll avoid similar issues</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Example Template</strong>:<br />
<em>"We acknowledge that our ads violated Google's Gambling Policy by promoting sports betting services without proper licensing. We have immediately paused all affected campaigns and removed the landing pages. Moving forward, we will only advertise in licensed jurisdictions and implement a pre-launch compliance review process."</em></p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Step 4: Upload Documents and Submit</h3>
<ul>
<li>File formats: PDF, JPG, PNG accepted</li>
<li>Size limit: Usually up to 10MB per file</li>
<li>File naming: Clearly label document type (e.g., <code>Business_License_2024.pdf</code>)</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>What to Do After Submitting</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Timeline</th>
<th>Action</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>1-3 business days</td>
<td>Check email for initial response</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>No response after 3-5 days</td>
<td>Submit follow-up or contact support</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Receive response</td>
<td>Read carefully and provide additional materials if requested</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Appeal approved</td>
<td>Reactivate account and test with small budget before scaling</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>⚠️ Quick FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q: Can I appeal again if rejected?</strong><br />
Yes, but wait 3-5 business days between attempts. Each appeal should include new materials or more detailed explanations.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Can I get a refund if my account is closed?</strong><br />
Balance is typically frozen during appeal. If appeal fails, you can request a refund to the original payment method.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Is using an agency more effective than self-appealing?</strong><br />
Not necessarily. Google applies uniform review standards, but agencies are more familiar with quick fixes for common violation types.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Key Actions: Before and After Appeal</h2>
<ul>
<li>🔍 <strong>Before</strong>: Confirm reason → Prepare materials → Fix violations</li>
<li>📝 <strong>During</strong>: Write structured statement → Name files clearly → Upload systematically</li>
<li>📊 <strong>After</strong>: Wait patiently → Follow up on emails → Test with small budget after approval</li>
</ul>
<p>There's no guarantee of 100% appeal success, but thorough preparation and genuine fixes significantly improve your chances. If your appeals keep failing, consider reevaluating your ad strategy and landing page compliance rather than repeatedly submitting the same materials.</p>
